<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanhadade Prabandha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1455</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kanhadade Prabandha is an epic poem in Old Western Rajasthani, the ancestor of Gujarati and Rajasthani, composed by the poet Padmanabha in 1455. It tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanhadadeva">Kanhadadeva</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalor</a>, and his resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> until the fall of Jalor in 1311, including the legend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s daughter and the prince Viramadeva.</p></div>
